[{"order":1,"type":"heading","text":"Jotai: Primitive and Flexible State Management in React"},{"order":2,"type":"paragraph","text":"Jotai is a minimalist, yet powerful state management library for React. It's built around the concept of 'atoms,' which are simple units of state that can be easily created, shared, and composed. Jotai distinguishes itself by its simplicity, flexibility, and performance, making it a great choice for both small and large React applications."},{"order":3,"type":"subheading","text":"Why Jotai?"},{"order":4,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Simplicity: Jotai's API is incredibly small and easy to learn. You primarily work with atoms and `useAtom`."},{"order":5,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Flexibility: Jotai doesn't impose a specific architecture. You can use it with any React component structure."},{"order":6,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Performance: Jotai is designed for optimal performance. It uses granular updates to re-render only the components that depend on the changed state."},{"order":7,"type":"bullet_point","text":"TypeScript Friendly: Jotai is written in TypeScript and provides excellent type safety."},{"order":8,"type":"subheading","text":"Getting Started"},{"order":9,"type":"paragraph","text":"First, install Jotai using npm or yarn:"},{"order":10,"type":"code","text":"npm install jotai
# or
yarn add jotai"},{"order":11,"type":"subheading","text":"Creating Your First Atom"},{"order":12,"type":"paragraph","text":"An atom is the fundamental unit of state in Jotai. You create an atom using the `atom` function:"},{"order":13,"type":"code","text":"import { atom } from 'jotai';

const countAtom = atom(0);"},{"order":14,"type":"paragraph","text":"In this example, `countAtom` is an atom that holds a number, initialized to 0."},{"order":15,"type":"subheading","text":"Using Atoms in Components"},{"order":16,"type":"paragraph","text":"To access and update the value of an atom within a React component, you use the `useAtom` hook:"},{"order":17,"type":"code","text":"import { useAtom } from 'jotai';

function Counter() {
  const [count, setCount] = useAtom(countAtom);

  return (
    <div>
      <p>Count: {count}</p>
      <button onClick={() => setCount(count + 1)}>Increment</button>
    </div>
  );
}

export default Counter;"},{"order":18,"type":"paragraph","text":"Here, `useAtom(countAtom)` returns an array containing the current value of the atom (`count`) and a function to update it (`setCount`).  When `setCount` is called, Jotai efficiently updates the component and any other components that use the same `countAtom`."},{"order":19,"type":"subheading","text":"Sharing Atoms Between Components"},{"order":20,"type":"paragraph","text":"Atoms can be easily shared between components. Simply import the atom into any component where you need to access or update its value:"},{"order":21,"type":"code","text":"// anotherComponent.js
import { useAtom } from 'jotai';
import { countAtom } from './Counter'; // Assuming Counter.js exports countAtom

function AnotherComponent() {
  const [count] = useAtom(countAtom);

  return (
    <div>
      <p>Current Count: {count}</p>
    </div>
  );
}"},{"order":22,"type":"paragraph","text":"Now, `AnotherComponent` will automatically re-render whenever the `countAtom`'s value changes, even if the change originates from the `Counter` component."},{"order":23,"type":"subheading","text":"Derived Atoms"},{"order":24,"type":"paragraph","text":"Jotai allows you to create derived atoms, which are atoms whose values are computed based on the values of other atoms. This is useful for creating complex state relationships."},{"order":25,"type":"code","text":"import { atom } from 'jotai';

const priceAtom = atom(10);
const quantityAtom = atom(2);

const totalAtom = atom((get) => get(priceAtom) * get(quantityAtom));

function TotalDisplay() {
  const [total] = useAtom(totalAtom);

  return (
    <div>
      <p>Total: ${total}</p>
    </div>
  );
}"},{"order":26,"type":"paragraph","text":"In this example, `totalAtom` is a derived atom that calculates the total price based on the `priceAtom` and `quantityAtom`. The function passed to `atom` receives a `get` function, which allows you to access the values of other atoms. When either `priceAtom` or `quantityAtom` changes, `totalAtom` will be automatically re-evaluated, and `TotalDisplay` will re-render."},{"order":27,"type":"subheading","text":"Writable Derived Atoms"},{"order":28,"type":"paragraph","text":"Derived atoms can also be writable, allowing you to update the underlying atoms based on the derived value. This is done by providing a second function to the `atom` function, which receives `get` and `set` functions."},{"order":29,"type":"code","text":"import { atom } from 'jotai';

const textAtom = atom('hello');

const upperCaseTextAtom = atom(
  (get) => get(textAtom).toUpperCase(),
  (get, set, update) => {
    set(textAtom, update.toLowerCase());
  }
);

function CaseConverter() {
  const [upperCaseText, setUpperCaseText] = useAtom(upperCaseTextAtom);

  return (
    <div>
      <p>Uppercase: {upperCaseText}</p>
      <button onClick={() => setUpperCaseText('NEW TEXT')}>Convert to Lowercase</button>
    </div>
  );
}"},{"order":30,"type":"paragraph","text":"In this example, `upperCaseTextAtom` is a writable derived atom. When `setUpperCaseText` is called, it updates the `textAtom` with the lowercase version of the new text."},{"order":31,"type":"subheading","text":"Atom Families"},{"order":32,"type":"paragraph","text":"Atom families allow you to create atoms dynamically based on a parameter. This is useful for managing state for lists or collections of items."},{"order":33,"type":"code","text":"import { atomFamily, useAtom } from 'jotai';

const todoAtomFamily = atomFamily((id) => atom({ id, text: '', completed: false }));

function TodoItem({ id }) {
  const [todo, setTodo] = useAtom(todoAtomFamily(id));

  return (
    <div>
      <input
        type="checkbox"
        checked={todo.completed}
        onChange={(e) => setTodo({ ...todo, completed: e.target.checked })} />
      <input
        type="text"
        value={todo.text}
        onChange={(e) => setTodo({ ...todo, text: e.target.value })} />
    </div>
  );
}

function TodoList() {
  const todoIds = [1, 2, 3]; // Example IDs

  return (
    <div>
      {todoIds.map((id) => (
        <TodoItem key={id} id={id} />
      ))}
    </div>
  );
}"},{"order":34,"type":"paragraph","text":"Here, `todoAtomFamily` creates a new atom for each todo item based on its `id`. Each `TodoItem` component uses `useAtom(todoAtomFamily(id))` to access and update the state for its specific todo item.  Jotai efficiently manages the state for each item independently."},{"order":35,"type":"subheading","text":"Asynchronous Actions"},{"order":36,"type":"paragraph","text":"Jotai works seamlessly with asynchronous actions. You can update atoms with the results of asynchronous operations."},{"order":37,"type":"code","text":"import { atom, useAtom } from 'jotai';

const dataAtom = atom(async () => {
  const response = await fetch('https://jsonplaceholder.typicode.com/todos/1');
  const data = await response.json();
  return data;
});

function DataDisplay() {
  const [data] = useAtom(dataAtom);

  if (data instanceof Promise) {
    return <p>Loading...</p>;
  }

  return (
    <div>
      <p>Title: {data.title}</p>
    </div>
  );
}"},{"order":38,"type":"paragraph","text":"In this example, `dataAtom` is initialized with an asynchronous function that fetches data from an API. Jotai automatically handles the promise and updates the component when the data is available. Note the check for `data instanceof Promise` to handle the loading state."},{"order":39,"type":"subheading","text":"Conclusion"},{"order":40,"type":"paragraph","text":"Jotai provides a simple, flexible, and performant way to manage state in React applications. Its minimalist API and granular updates make it a great choice for projects of any size. By understanding the core concepts of atoms, derived atoms, and atom families, you can effectively leverage Jotai to build robust and maintainable React applications."}]
